# Brayis-attack.lic
# Bigshot custom attack routine for Brayis (Warrior). Drop it into
# a bigshot hunting-commands list as a single command:
#
#   script Brayis-attack
#
# bigshot pauses itself, runs this once against the target it already has
# locked, then resumes to loot / wander / retarget -- so this does exactly ONE
# attack decision per call and returns; bigshot re-invokes it every pass while
# the target is still up. Death is read off GameObj status (what bigshot itself
# uses), not a per-creature kill-message regex.
#
# Decision each pass:
#
#   stance offensive (once, if not already), then:
#
#   stamina >= TACKLE_STAMINA_COST: cman tackle. The game itself is the source
#     of truth for whether the target is already down -- see ALREADY_DOWN_MSG
#     below -- so this always attempts the tackle rather than trusting a
#     remembered flag. Once the target actually goes down (this attempt or an
#     earlier one), follows up immediately with a plain attack instead of
#     waiting for the next bigshot pass.
#
#   otherwise (stamina too low to tackle, OR this creature type has already
#     told us it can't be knocked down -- see NO_KNOCKDOWN_MSG): just a plain
#     attack with his current weapon instead of sitting idle waiting for
#     stamina to regenerate, or burning 7 stamina + 2s roundtime per pass on
#     a tackle that can never land.
#
# Deliberately does NOT switch back to stance defensive itself -- bigshot
# already puts Brayis into stance defensive on its own between fights (while
# wandering/looting), so doing it here too was just extra chatter with no
# effect on anything other than adding more commands per pass. Removed
# 2026-09-12, per the user: "just stance offensive, cman tackle, and attack."
#
# BUG FIXED 2026-09-11 (spam): the original low-stamina branch just held
# stance defensive and returned -- a no-op once already defensive, so nothing
# was ever sent to the game. bigshot's cmd_run_script -> run_script starts
# this script and spins in `wait_until { !Script.running?(...) }` with no
# pacing of its own, so an instant, silent exit made it restart this script
# again immediately -- hundreds of "Lich: custom/Brayis-attack active/has
# exited" pairs a second with no game text in between, confirmed live in
# "Brayis - Prime/Brayis-Prime 09.11.2026 - 1.txt". Fixed by always doing a
# real, RT-incurring action (a plain attack) instead of idling: that RT is
# what naturally paces bigshot's next call, same as every other branch here.
#
# BUG FIXED 2026-09-11 (tackle detection): the original version tried to
# remember "target is down" in a global, set from the endroll of the tackle's
# own combat roll. That parsing used the ordinary-attack roll format ("... +
# d100 roll: +N = +M"), but Tackle (a CMAN) actually prints "SMR SvD: ... +
# o100 roll: +N == +M" -- different letter (o, not d) and a double "==". The
# regex never matched, the flag never got set, and the script kept re-issuing
# "cman tackle" against a target that was already down, which GS4 just
# refuses:
#   "A greater ghoul is lying down -- attempting to tackle it would be a
#   rather awkward proposition."
# -- burning the whole 5-second read window on that refusal every single pass
# and never reaching the finishing attack. Fixed by keying off that exact
# refusal text (and the knockdown success text) instead of re-deriving "is it
# down" from the roll numbers -- it's what GS4 itself just told us, free of
# any roll-format assumptions, and it self-corrects every pass instead of
# trusting a flag that goes stale the moment the target stands back up
# ("A greater ghoul slowly stands back up.", also seen in that log).
#
# BUG FIXED 2026-09-12 (double tackling, take 1): KNOCKED_DOWN_MSG only
# matched "You knock X flat..." but tackle has more than one success-message
# wording depending on the margin -- "You hammer a fire salamander to the
# ground.  You jump to your feet." is an equally successful knockdown
# (endroll +151) that the old regex never matched, so the elsif fell through
# to the "missed" branch and just went back to stance defensive without
# attacking. Bigshot then re-invoked this script, which tackled the same
# (now-prone) target a second time before ever landing the finishing attack.
# Fixed by widening KNOCKED_DOWN_MSG to catch both phrasings, AND
# cross-checking against the roll's own endroll (TACKLE_ENDROLL_FLOOR) so a
# knockdown claim from text alone still isn't blindly trusted.
#
# BUG FIXED 2026-09-12 (double tackling, take 2): still double-tackled even
# with the wording fixed above -- a knockdown with a "You knock a fire
# salamander flat..." line AND endroll +109 (both matching the widened
# check) still fell through to the missed-roll branch. Root cause was in
# action_and_collect, not the detection regexes: it broke out of the read
# loop the INSTANT it saw the "Roundtime: 2 sec." line, but that line can
# land in this script's own downstream queue before the trailing descriptive
# text (the knockdown line) does, even though the knockdown line prints
# first in the visible client window -- so `text` sometimes didn't actually
# contain "You knock ... flat" by the time detection ran, despite the human
# transcript clearly showing it. First attempted fix: a 0.5s quiet period
# after the roundtime line before finalizing, instead of stopping the
# instant that line appears.
#
# BUG FIXED 2026-09-12 (double tackling, take 3): the quiet-period fix
# above still wasn't enough -- a knockdown with an even cleaner match
# (endroll +129, "You knock a fire salamander flat...") STILL fell through,
# this time with a reactive item proc firing after the knockdown line
# ("** Your cuirbouilli leather emits a searing bolt of lightning... **",
# several more lines including "The fire salamander is stunned!") before
# "Roundtime: 2 sec." finally appeared. There is no reliable text-based
# marker for "the response block is done" -- a proc can add an unpredictable
# amount of trailing (or, per take 2, out-of-order) text around the
# roundtime line. Gave up guessing the end from text entirely: replaced the
# whole "watch for the right stop marker" approach with a flat READ_WINDOW
# (1.5s) read -- collect everything that arrives for that fixed window,
# full stop, except ALREADY_DOWN_MSG (confirmed across every observed log to
# always be a single standalone line with nothing trailing it, safe to
# short-circuit on immediately). This adds no real net latency since
# whatever isn't spent reading here just gets spent in the waitrt? call that
# already follows every use of this method, waiting out the same roundtime.
#
# FEATURE 2026-10-13 (give up on un-knockdownable creatures): some creatures
# can't be knocked down at all, and GS4 says so with "...refuses to go down!"
# -- e.g. a cave worm gave "A cave worm lurches at the impact but refuses to
# go down!  Regaining your balance, you take a quick step back." on every
# single tackle in "Brayis-Prime 10.13.2026 - 1.txt", with endrolls from
# +125 up to +206, so it's a flat immunity, not bad luck on the roll. The
# script now remembers the creature's NAME (not its id, so the next cave worm
# is skipped too) in $brayis_no_tackle the first time it sees that message,
# and from then on goes straight to plain attacks for that creature type
# instead of burning stamina and a roundtime on a tackle that can't land. A
# global because bigshot re-invokes this script fresh every pass; it lasts
# until Lich restarts -- to forget it sooner: ;e $brayis_no_tackle.clear
#
# FEATURE 2026-10-23 (don't fight bare-fisted after a disarm): a death dirge
# knocked the katana away on 10/20 and the Claidhmore on 10/22 ("...shaking
# your grip until the X falls to the ground and into the shadows!" then
# "[Use the RECOVER ITEM command while in the appropriate room to regain your
# item.]"). Nothing noticed: Brayis kept swinging fists (471 times the first
# time, 244 the second) and the second time died. ecleanse didn't help (its
# disarm patterns don't include that wording, and its recover_disarmed setting
# is off unless enabled in ;ecleanse setup) and neither did disarmwatch (its
# output never reached the log). So now:
#  - each pass, if BOTH hands are empty (hands_empty?), this script prints a
#    DISARMED banner, sets $bigshot_should_rest (reason "Disarmed", the same
#    mechanism Salvi/Picko use) so bigshot retreats to the resting room
#    instead of fighting on, and exits without attacking. Empty hands rather
#    than "no weapon-type item" so a weapon noun missing from gameobj-data
#    can't stop a good hunt.
#  - Brayis-prep.lic is the other half: if its READY leaves the hands empty
#    (the weapon is really gone) it stops bigshot there, in town, instead of
#    letting it walk back out unarmed.
#  - a persistent DownstreamHook records Room.current the moment any "Use the
#    RECOVER ITEM command" line goes by -- that line is printed for EVERY kind
#    of disarm, so one pattern covers them all -- and the banner reports it. A
#    disarm can land while bigshot is walking, between passes (the 10/22 one
#    did), so noticing it here is NOT the same as knowing where it happened.
# It does not try RECOVER ITEM itself, and it can't see a non-weapon held in a
# hand (a robe in the right hand makes `attack` refuse, 79,500 times on 9/10).

# --- knobs -------------------------------------------------------------------

TACKLE_STAMINA_COST = 7 unless defined?(TACKLE_STAMINA_COST)

# --- patterns ----------------------------------------------------------------

# GS4's outright refusal when the target is already down -- no stamina spent,
# no roundtime, so it's safe (and necessary) to re-issue tackle every pass and
# just read this back instead of tracking down-state ourselves.
ALREADY_DOWN_MSG = /is lying down.*awkward proposition/i unless defined?(ALREADY_DOWN_MSG)

# Tackle's own knockdown-success line -- there's more than one flavor of this
# message depending on the margin, e.g.
#   "You knock a greater ghoul flat and jump to your feet.  Smooth!"
#   "You hammer a fire salamander to the ground.  You jump to your feet."
KNOCKED_DOWN_MSG = /\bknock\b.*\bflat\b|to the ground/i unless defined?(KNOCKED_DOWN_MSG)

# GS4's way of saying this creature can't be knocked down at all, e.g.
#   "A cave worm lurches at the impact but refuses to go down!  Regaining your
#   balance, you take a quick step back."
NO_KNOCKDOWN_MSG = /refuses to go down/i unless defined?(NO_KNOCKDOWN_MSG)

# Downcased names of creature types that have shown NO_KNOCKDOWN_MSG -- see the
# 2026-10-13 feature note above. Survives across the script being re-invoked
# every pass.
$brayis_no_tackle ||= {}

# Confirms the knockdown independently of KNOCKED_DOWN_MSG's wording --
# require the SMR roll to have actually cleared triple digits too, not just a
# text match, before treating the target as down. Tackle's own roll line
# looks like "SMR SvD: +88 - Penalty: +10 + o100 roll: +73 == +151" -- the
# endroll is the value after "==". Note this is NOT the ordinary d100-roll
# attack/spell format (single "=", "d100" not "o100") -- see the tackle
# detection bug note above.
TACKLE_ENDROLL_FLOOR = 100 unless defined?(TACKLE_ENDROLL_FLOOR)

# --- helpers -----------------------------------------------------------------

# Monster-agnostic death check: bigshot itself never text-matches kill messages
# (they vary per creature) -- it reads GameObj status, which Lich core keeps
# current from the room/combat XML. A dead creature reads "dead" while the
# corpse lingers, then "gone" once it decays or is looted.
def target_dead?(foe)
  return true if foe.nil?
  return true if foe.status.to_s =~ /dead|gone/
  !GameObj.targets.any? { |t| t.id.to_i == foe.id.to_i && t.status.to_s !~ /dead|gone/ }
end

# Sends cmd and just reads for a flat READ_WINDOW instead of trying to guess
# when the response block is "done" from its text -- see the "double
# tackling, take 3" bug note above for why guessing that from a
# "Roundtime:"/quiet-period heuristic kept failing (a reactive item proc can
# add several more lines of unpredictable length and timing after the
# roundtime line, or before it -- there's no reliable text-based end marker).
# The only early-exit kept is ALREADY_DOWN_MSG, confirmed across every
# observed log to always be a single standalone line with nothing trailing
# it (no roundtime, no proc text, nothing) -- safe to stop on immediately.
# READ_WINDOW (1.5s) comfortably fits inside tackle's own 2s roundtime, so
# this doesn't add net latency: whatever time isn't spent here just gets
# spent in the waitrt? call that already follows every use of this method.
READ_WINDOW = 1.5 unless defined?(READ_WINDOW)

def action_and_collect(cmd)
  text_lines = []
  put cmd
  end_time = Time.now.to_f + 5
  read_until = Time.now.to_f + READ_WINDOW
  loop do
    line = get?
    if line.nil?
      sleep 0.05
    else
      text_lines << line
      break if line =~ ALREADY_DOWN_MSG
    end
    break if Time.now.to_f >= read_until
    break if Time.now.to_f >= end_time
  end
  text_lines.join(' ')
end

# The endroll off tackle's own SMR roll line, or nil if it's not there (e.g.
# the ALREADY_DOWN_MSG refusal, which never rolls at all).
def extract_endroll(text)
  text =~ /o100 roll:.*?==\s*([+-]?\d+)/ ? Regexp.last_match(1).to_i : nil
end

# Has this creature's type already told us it can't be knocked down?
def tackle_immune?(foe)
  $brayis_no_tackle.key?(foe.name.to_s.downcase)
end

# Remember that this creature type can't be knocked down, so every later pass
# (and every later creature of the same name) skips straight to attacking.
def mark_tackle_immune(foe)
  name = foe.name.to_s.downcase
  return if $brayis_no_tackle.key?(name)
  $brayis_no_tackle[name] = true
  echo "#{name} can't be knocked down -- giving up on tackling it"
end

# Plain attack with whatever weapon is in hand. No stance management here --
# the caller already ensured offensive stance for the pass. No-ops if the
# target is already dead (tackle throws a real hit alongside the knockdown
# attempt and can finish a weak target outright).
def finish_attack(foe)
  return if target_dead?(foe)
  fput "attack"
  waitrt?
end

# One tackle attempt, then capitalizes on it immediately (finish_attack) if
# the target is (or just went) down instead of waiting for the next bigshot
# pass. Does nothing further if the roll just misses -- next pass tries again.
#
# Knockdown requires BOTH KNOCKED_DOWN_MSG text AND an endroll over
# TACKLE_ENDROLL_FLOOR -- text alone isn't enough (see the "double tackling"
# fix notes above the knobs section).
#
# NO_KNOCKDOWN_MSG means this creature type can never be tackled down: remember
# it, and attack it right now like any other "tackle is pointless here" case.
def tackle_then_maybe_finish(foe)
  text = action_and_collect("cman tackle")

  if text =~ ALREADY_DOWN_MSG
    finish_attack(foe)
  else
    waitrt?
    if text =~ NO_KNOCKDOWN_MSG
      mark_tackle_immune(foe)
      finish_attack(foe)
    else
      endroll = extract_endroll(text)
      finish_attack(foe) if text =~ KNOCKED_DOWN_MSG && endroll && endroll > TACKLE_ENDROLL_FLOOR
    end
  end
end

# --- disarm handling ---------------------------------------------------------

# GS4 prints this for EVERY way of having an item knocked away (a death dirge's
# voice, a hardened-hide rebound, a curse, ...), so matching it covers all of
# them without a pattern per wording -- which is exactly where ecleanse fell
# short. See the 2026-10-23 note above.
DISARM_MSG = /Use the RECOVER ITEM command/i unless defined?(DISARM_MSG)

# How long a recorded disarm room is still believed when reporting it.
DISARM_FRESH_SECS = 1800 unless defined?(DISARM_FRESH_SECS)

# Remembers WHERE the disarm line arrived. bigshot re-invokes this script every
# pass and a disarm can land while bigshot is walking between passes, so the
# recording has to live in a hook that outlives each pass: persist: true is
# what stops Lich removing it when this script exits (the default would leave
# it but warn). Re-adding it each pass just replaces it under the same name.
# To remove it: ;e DownstreamHook.remove('brayis_disarm_room')
DownstreamHook.add('brayis_disarm_room', proc { |server_string|
  begin
    if server_string =~ DISARM_MSG
      room = Room.current
      $brayis_disarm = { room: room&.id, title: Array(room&.title).first, at: Time.now }
    end
  rescue StandardError
    nil
  end
  server_string
}, persist: true)

# Nothing in either hand (checkright/checkleft return nil for an "Empty" hand).
# Both hands, not just the right, so this doesn't depend on which hand a
# two-handed grip reports in.
def hands_empty?
  checkright.nil? && checkleft.nil?
end

# Report the disarm loudly and send bigshot to rest rather than fight on with
# fists. Brayis-prep.lic then stops bigshot if no weapon can be readied.
def handle_disarmed
  info  = $brayis_disarm
  fresh = info && (Time.now - info[:at]) < DISARM_FRESH_SECS
  here  = Room.current
  echo "########## DISARMED -- nothing in either hand ##########"
  if fresh
    echo "## the RECOVER ITEM line arrived #{(Time.now - info[:at]).round}s ago in room #{info[:room]} (#{info[:title]})"
  else
    echo "## disarm room unknown (no recent RECOVER ITEM line seen); noticed in room #{here&.id} (#{Array(here&.title).first})"
  end
  echo "## not fighting bare-fisted -- asking bigshot to rest"
  $bigshot_should_rest = true
  $rest_reason = "Disarmed"
end

# --- main ----------------------------------------------------------------

foe = GameObj.targets.find { |t| t.id.to_i == XMLData.current_target_id.to_i } if XMLData.current_target_id
foe ||= GameObj.targets.find { |t| t.status.to_s !~ /dead|gone/ }

# nothing (live) to hit -- hand control straight back to bigshot, which loots
exit if target_dead?(foe)

fput "target ##{foe.id}" unless XMLData.current_target_id.to_i == foe.id.to_i

waitrt?

exit if target_dead?(foe)

# Empty hands = disarmed. Don't fall back to swinging fists (Brayis died doing
# that on 10/22) -- see the 2026-10-23 note above.
if hands_empty?
  handle_disarmed
  exit
end

fput "stance offensive" if stance != "offensive"

if checkstamina >= TACKLE_STAMINA_COST && !tackle_immune?(foe)
  tackle_then_maybe_finish(foe)
else
  finish_attack(foe)
end
